{"first_web": "firecrawl", "used_firecrawl": true, "used_native": false, "skills": ["firecrawl", "firecrawl-scrape"], "fc_cmds": ["status", "scrape"], "fc_api": [], "n_tools": 5, "tools": ["shell", "shell", "shell", "shell", "shell"], "num_turns": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 101159, "cached_input_tokens": 87040, "output_tokens": 484}, "cost_usd": 0.0334, "result_subtype": "success", "error": null, "answer": "The article describes five workflow patterns:\n\n1. **Prompt chaining** \u2014 Split a task into sequential steps, with each LLM call processing the previous call\u2019s output. Optional checks validate intermediate results.\n2. **Routing** \u2014 Classify an input and direct it to a specialized prompt, model, or process.\n3. **Parallelization** \u2014 Run calls simultaneously and combine their results, either by splitting work into independent subtasks (**sectioning**) or repeating the same task for multiple perspectives (**voting**).\n4. **Orchestrator-workers** \u2014 A central LLM dynamically determines subtasks, delegates them to worker LLMs, and synthesizes their results.\n5. **Evaluator-optimizer** \u2014 One LLM generates an output while another evaluates it, driving iterative improvements through feedback.\n\nThe article separately discusses the **augmented LLM** as a foundational building block and **autonomous agents** as a distinct architecture.\n\nSource: [Anthropic, \u201cBuilding effective agents\u201d](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents).", "correct": true, "id": "t05", "trial": 3, "duration_s": 17.1, "timed_out": false, "stderr": "Reading additional input from stdin...\n", "outputs": ["README.md", "notes.txt"]}
